WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.977
- I got an email from an
energy company that said,

00:00:02.977 --> 00:00:07.200
"Hi, Tom. We own a wind turbine.
Do you want to go up to the top of it?"

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.190
And of course I said yes,
but then I asked,

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:13.210
could I leave by the emergency exit?

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:16.460
And for some reason they said yes.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.748
- This is a 50 metre EWT
turbine, which is capable

00:00:19.748 --> 00:00:21.986
of producing 500 kilowatts
of energy.

00:00:21.986 --> 00:00:25.477
In blade repair, we have to repair
turbines all over Europe.

00:00:25.477 --> 00:00:28.600
So, every day you need to climb a turbine.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:30.353
You shut it off at the bottom.
You climb up.

00:00:30.353 --> 00:00:31.186
You pin the turbine

00:00:31.186 --> 00:00:33.260
and pin the blades that
you're going to work on.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.950
You're going to attach
to the top, abseil out,

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:38.500
and do your repairs on the blades
that need repairing.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.510
- Anyone climbing or
descending this ladder

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:43.140
has to be attached to
a fall arrest system.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.280
It's a clever bit of technology.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.700
If you move at a normal speed,
it'll move along with you.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:50.160
But if you fall, it'll notice
and it'll catch you.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.051
Fast enough to be safe,

00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:54.710
not so fast that it's as brutal
as hitting the ground would be.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:56.761
- On the ladder that we
climb up on the inside,

00:00:56.761 --> 00:00:59.330
there is a fixed steel cable.

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:00.950
And on this, you attach a slider,

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:03.830
which is a metal device and
you open up a gate inside.

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:04.688
And this is where the cable runs

00:01:04.688 --> 00:01:07.080
and the gate then closes onto the cable.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.305
Shut the hatch, screw it together.

00:01:09.305 --> 00:01:11.101
And this attaches to your sternum.

00:01:11.101 --> 00:01:14.900
So as you're climbing up, this
will follow you up the cable.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:17.140
And then if you fell,
it would lock into position.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.160
It'll brake.
That's your braking system.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:20.780
You're always connected by two points.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.679
Personally, I believe it's a safer job
than anything else you can do

00:01:23.679 --> 00:01:25.590
because of all the backups we use.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.370
For instance, it's much more
dangerous to climb a ladder

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:29.371
six foot off the ground

00:01:29.371 --> 00:01:30.890
than it is to work 50 metres up in the air

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:33.300
when you've got all
your attachment points.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.550
In my opinion.

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:37.428
- If you're climbing this
turbine, you need a fail safe.

00:01:37.428 --> 00:01:40.943
Which is one of the reasons
there's an emergency exit at the top.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:42.863
Right, here we go.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:46.720
- The emergency descenders
come in different guises

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:47.553
but the one that's at the top there,

00:01:47.553 --> 00:01:51.680
we only train with it
once every two years,

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.830
because it's always the worst case scenario
if you have to get that out.

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.210
It's an inertia device.
If you just clip it to yourself,

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.780
you'll lower down to the
ground under its own steam,

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.880
and you can brake but, generally,
you'll just hit the ground at a safe speed.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:06.560
You don't train with it very often
because in real world

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:07.393
you should never have to use it

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:09.820
because you've got lots of
other things to fail first,

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:11.461
before you even have to
think about using that.

00:02:11.461 --> 00:02:13.670
There's instructions written on it
as well, on how to use it.

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:15.820
- So step to the left?
- Take a step round here, yeah.

00:02:16.653 --> 00:02:17.486
- To access a turbine,

00:02:17.486 --> 00:02:20.561
you need to do something called a GWO,
"Global Wind Organisation",

00:02:20.561 --> 00:02:22.625
and you can't access the turbine

00:02:22.625 --> 00:02:25.270
and use any of the equipment inside
without that training.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:28.189
That teaches about working at height
and how to use one of those devices

00:02:28.189 --> 00:02:30.000
to get the casualties down.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.890
And then to do what we do,
which is abseiling on the blades,

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:35.192
You need to do something
called a IRATA qualification,

00:02:35.192 --> 00:02:36.810
which is rope access, basically.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:40.173
And so it's quite a lengthy process
before you're even out there.

00:02:40.173 --> 00:02:43.040
- There is actually an
emergency exit sign up here.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:43.945
I love that.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:48.910
- This is a direct drive turbine,
meaning that there's no gear in it.

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:52.640
It's all just magnets up in that doughnut,
you can see right at the top.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.473
- So, this is the centre of the turbine.

00:02:55.473 --> 00:03:01.350
- That there is the whole rotor.
It's a giant electromagnet.

00:03:02.183 --> 00:03:04.500
There are very few worst case
scenarios where

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:07.800
the turbine is going to be fine,
but the ladder down isn't.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:09.490
Now, if any of those happen,
then yes,

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:11.510
the team here are all
thoroughly trained for it.

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.370
They'll all be able to get down
on the emergency descender.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:16.210
But that's not why it's here.

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:17.977
The most likely reason
for that to be used

00:03:17.977 --> 00:03:19.890
is for one person.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:23.472
Somebody who's twisted their ankle
or passed out, or for whatever reason,

00:03:23.472 --> 00:03:26.220
can't get down
the ladder on their own.

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:32.810
- Yes, that's my understanding.
It would be specific to the injury.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:34.410
If we couldn't move the casualty,

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:36.110
then they would have to
figure a way of doing that.

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:37.452
You'd have to be something
serious.

00:03:37.452 --> 00:03:39.930
A heart attack,
you can always get someone down.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:43.380
But if someone had a broken neck,
yeah, it will be a different scenario.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:44.473
- Oh, look at that view!

00:03:46.643 --> 00:03:47.880
- We do medicals
to get onto the turbines

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:50.660
and we have to maintain
those medicals bi-yearly.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:52.890
And that proves your fitness
to climb the turbines

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:54.120
and medicals are quite in-depth.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:55.400
So if you knew you were ill,

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:58.470
you would be fairly irresponsible
to go and climb a turbine.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:00.240
- Yep. That's okay to step on?

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.710
- Yeah. Promise you! If you sit back,
legs straight and lean backwards.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:04.810
Keep going, keep going.

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:05.643
Don't hold on.

00:04:05.643 --> 00:04:06.548
- Oh!

00:04:06.548 --> 00:04:07.689
- Don't hold on.

00:04:07.689 --> 00:04:08.522
- Oh. Oh, okay.

00:04:08.522 --> 00:04:10.430
That's the nerve-wracking bit.

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:11.460
- That's it, keep going.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.530
- Yep. I've got two wires on me.

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:15.690
I've got two wires on me.
I've got two wires on me.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:17.740
- And now walk, but
don't bend in your legs.

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:18.573
That's it.

00:04:18.573 --> 00:04:19.406
Keep going.

00:04:21.485 --> 00:04:22.318
- Okay. I've got two wires on me.

00:04:22.318 --> 00:04:24.432
- And you can slowly bend your knees
a little bit if you want to.

00:04:24.432 --> 00:04:25.265
- I've got two wires.

00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:26.200
That's fine.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:27.033
Okay.

00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:27.866
- Happy?

00:04:27.866 --> 00:04:29.260
- Happy!

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:30.464
- See you in a little while.

00:04:30.464 --> 00:04:32.070
- Okay!

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:34.500
"One thing I should mention in voiceover,
because I forgot to say it

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:37.810
"while convincing my brain to switch
from trusting my legs to trusting the ropes:

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:39.820
"there is a way to get someone
onto the descender

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:41.940
"if they're out cold or they can't walk.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:43.520
"It's just that I could walk.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:46.160
"So I did a standard abseil
down the first part."

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.210
And that's it. I'm descending!

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:52.527
Rigging this up required a
lot of skill and training

00:04:52.527 --> 00:04:54.068
from the folks up there.

00:04:54.068 --> 00:04:57.630
But to descend, you don't
even need to be conscious.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.438
And that's by design.

00:04:59.438 --> 00:05:05.186
All I need to do now is sit and wait
until I safely reach the ground.

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:10.330
- When you actually work on a turbine,
the view at the top is always amazing.

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:13.120
So when you have a look
around, you do remember that

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:14.870
oh, it's pretty cool job, actually.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:20.110
- Thank you very much to all the team
at Octopus Energy and at EWT.

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:22.410
You can find links to them
in the description.

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:26.220
That's a wrap. Thank you very much!

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:27.053
- Well done!

